fn section_2() -> RoadmapSection {
  RoadmapSection {
    title: "2. Compute fabric — own the infra (CI · deploy · control plane)",
    elements: [
      section_prose(content: "State (2026-07-01): required CI is back on the fleet after the Ubicloud detour (#6107, reverted #6111). The detour was the **diagnostic and emergency valve, not a failure**: it proved raw VM isolation alone does not solve a serial floor (same ~50min wall on both runner types), and it stays the **break-glass/burst option**, just not the active required path. So the active work is (1) reduce floor recompute, (2) make fleet admission/caps safe — the dominant wall-clock is inside the floor, not host placement alone. The fleet — srv1/srv2 (128c/125GiB Ampere) + srv3 (greenfield, OpenBMC) — carries agent sessions, the dashboard, and all runners, with live cgroup caps, runner registration, sudoers, and tailscale-ACL actuation still **hand-managed**; the fleet-era OOM/sccache exposure returned with the traffic (operator-accepted interim). #6096 (fleet converge control plane) was **closed unmerged**; its review gap-list is the re-land spec, and the findings apply to the committed `.github/fleet-converge.sh` too (mutate-then-read, `|| true` swallowing, no hostname dispatch) — the re-land is an architecture change, not a rebase. → [CI humming](docs/plans/ci-humming.md) · [host-effect orchestration](docs/plans/host-effect-orchestration.md) · [compute envelope](docs/plans/compute-envelope-model.md) · [charter](docs/plans/ci-process-end-to-end.md) · [host-converge inventory](docs/plans/host-converge-inventory.md)"),
      section_prose(content: "**The core design rule (operator-signed, 2026-07-01):** provider-specific power is earned behind a `ProviderOffer` adapter; the core fabric only knows `RunShape → Allocation → Receipt`; default allocation is **strict/ephemeral**; oversubscription is an optimization proven by receipts, never assumed by placement. Not \"Ubicloud vs fleet\" — GitHub Actions asks for a RunShape, the fabric chooses a ProviderOffer (srv1/srv2 fixed-host slice, Ubicloud ephemeral VM, or later others). srv1/srv2 are modeled as little Ubiclouds first (N strict slots, hard caps, read-back receipts); their richer allocation opportunity (stranded cores, burst, co-residency) is Phase 2, earned by receipts."),
      section_prose(content: "**◆ Milestones:** OOM root-caused (hand-set caps vs measured peaks + budget double-count) ✓ · Ubicloud stabilization trialed ✓ (#6107) → reverted, operator call (#6111 — no wall-clock win; width=9 was live by construction and never touched the serial wall) · converge shell emitted ✓ (#5725/#5827) · `apply()` Phase A ✓ (#5756) · CD to srv1 ✓ (#6030/#6060/#6093) · witness-level affected-set pruning LIVE ✓ (#6061) → **▸ NOW: kill the serial compile-clean wall · floor <1min · CD wrong-host fix · stateless-frontend MVP (first non-CI fabric consumer) · converge re-land to the signed design** → srv1/srv2 runner-allocation v0 (hand-config retired) → StrictLease + shape labels → dormant-Ubicloud provider row (design-break probe) → periodic actuation with receipts → host lifecycle/decom on the same spine → **TERMINAL: required CI runs on the compute fabric — the model can choose any admitted ProviderOffer, the receipt proves the shape was honored, and install/manage/decom all ride `apply()` with fail-closed receipts**"),
      section_prose(content: "**Acceptance rule (operator, 2026-07-02): an item is not complete because its algebra exists — it is complete only when a NAMED consumer uses it on the LIVE path and produces a receipt** (DESIGN §5: a typecheck or a grep is not a consumer). Completion tiers: **T0** modeled · **T1** fixture/synthetic witness · **T2** wired to the intended consumer (proof by consumption, never grep) · **T3** live dry-run receipt · **T4** live apply + independent read-back receipt · **T5** a periodic/CI consumer keeps it alive. Every dispatchable node states **Accept:** — its required tier plus an operator-checkable end state on the real fleet (what is different on srv1/srv2 or in CI afterward) plus a discriminating RED; control-plane/fabric items close at **T4/T5**, and anything below the named tier is a subtask, never completion. A sized node without an explicit Accept is not ready to dispatch; algebra, carriers, and lenses are mechanisms and never their own acceptance. The bar for real progress: *can srv1/srv2 runner allocation be changed through the model, read back, re-run safely, and retracted?* Until yes, the work is scaffolding. Full per-node checklists: [fleet acceptance criteria](docs/plans/fleet-acceptance-criteria.md) — the node's Accept line is the summary, the doc is the checklist, and edits to one land with the other."),
      section_group(
        label: "Now — correctness of the current posture",
        nodes: [
          owned(rn: ticket_wi(id: "2-cd-transport", done: true, t: TicketFields {
            headline: "fix the CD transport premise — a deploy needs a target-host proof",
            brief: "deploy_dashboard_srv1 is a srv1 EFFECT, but job placement is not an srv1 PROOF: LocalShell with principal runner assumed the runner is ON srv1 (#6093), while [self-hosted, linux, arm64] can land on srv2 (and during the Ubicloud window it was a rented VM). A LocalShell deploy is valid ONLY when the runner identity proves it is already on srv1 — pin the job to an srv1-specific trusted runner label, or make the effect SshShell-over-tailnet with an explicit srv1 target + typed credential (#6066 Phase 2).",
            displaced_cost: "every main push gambling the deploy host",
            current_state: "",
            first_slice: "",
            red_control: "",
            out_of_scope: "",
            handback: "",
            updates: [
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-25", note: "migrated to ticket form (W2-bulk tranche 17)" },
            ],
            authored_on: "",
            last_verified_on: "",
          }, intricacy: IntricacyHigh, volume: VolumeSmall, repo: "gunbc"), owner: "dispatched 2026-07-01 · adhoc-75790c4c-4e0"),
          ticket_wi(id: "2-admission-model", done: false, t: TicketFields {
            headline: "workload-class admission model",
            brief: "Runners are capacity surfaces, not concurrency proof. Define CI workload classes (floor_light · rust_heavy · deploy · session) with measured RAM/swap/pids/CPU-token demand; admitted concurrency per host DERIVES from HostSupply × WorkloadClassDemand, never from runner-slot count. Initial 128c/125GiB policy: ~42 build tokens per host (≈1 core : 3 GiB), with intentionally-stranded cores reserved for sessions and low-memory work — stranded is not wasted. Measured anchors: ci ~11GiB · rust_tests ~37GiB peaks vs the old 8G slot caps.",
            displaced_cost: "",
            current_state: "",
            first_slice: "",
            red_control: "Accept: admitted concurrency for srv1/srv2 derives by execution from HostSupply × WorkloadClassDemand (witnessed, not hand-set), and a proposed allocation whose Σ exceeds supply is REFUSED with a typed verdict — perturb: inflate one class's measured demand and admission shrinks",
            out_of_scope: "",
            handback: "",
            updates: [
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-01", note: "operator directive: 1 core → 3 GiB + swap" },
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-25", note: "migrated to ticket form (W2-bulk tranche 16)" },
            ],
            authored_on: "2026-07-01",
            last_verified_on: "",
          }, intricacy: IntricacyHigh, volume: VolumeMedium, repo: "gunbc"),
          ticket_row(id: "2-cap-deconflation", done: false, t: TicketFields {
            headline: "cap/admission de-conflation",
            brief: "The three facts now live in three mechanisms — per-slot containment = cgroup caps via converge (#7009/#7000) · host admission = fleet_host_budget conservation (host_allocation_conserves) · build width = the runtime AIMD memory governor (the 2026-07-12 adaptive-width ruling, which retired the width folds and per-shard byte predictions the original framing assumed).",
            displaced_cost: "",
            current_state: "re-based 2026-07-23",
            first_slice: "a receipt that the three mechanisms cover the original knobs with no conflated survivor, or re-home the residue",
            red_control: "",
            out_of_scope: "",
            handback: "",
            updates: [
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-25", note: "migrated to ticket form (W2-bulk tranche 16)" },
            ],
            authored_on: "",
            last_verified_on: "2026-07-23",
          }),
          ticket_pp(id: "2-merge-admission", done: false, t: TicketFields {
            headline: "merge-admission gate: close the HELD state",
            brief: "Wired (#5974/#6080) but the freshness block is HELD pending a real green-on-main receipt, and the #6080→#6101 accidental re-apply/re-revert needs a clean landing.",
            displaced_cost: "stale-base merges landed BOTH the .gitignore drift (healed #6110/#6111) AND the extdeps.shell collision that kept main red — green-on-branch is not green-on-main",
            current_state: "HELD",
            first_slice: "",
            red_control: "",
            out_of_scope: "",
            handback: "",
            updates: [
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-25", note: "migrated to ticket form (W2-bulk tranche 17)" },
            ],
            authored_on: "",
            last_verified_on: "",
          }, carriers: [ptr(label: "decision record", path: "docs/plans/ci-merge-freshness.md")]),
          ticket_wi(id: "2-stateless-frontend", done: false, t: TicketFields {
            headline: "stateless frontend MVP on fabric",
            brief: "Milestone (A) LANDED: roadmap static site served on srv1 (#6128) + healthz REST GET readiness edge (#6753) + bounded read-back (#6769) + roadmap_site_healthz_validate; live_deploy apply/retract on the membership spine (#6814/#6940/#7011).",
            displaced_cost: "",
            current_state: "REMAINING: milestone (B) fabric SiteLease on the same allocation model as CI (waits on the control-plane read-back); the public-domain discharge (gunb.ai Pages cutover) is assigned to the site-subsumption lane and has NOT merged yet",
            first_slice: "",
            red_control: "",
            out_of_scope: "",
            handback: "",
            updates: [
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-25", note: "migrated to ticket form (W2-bulk tranche 18)" },
            ],
            authored_on: "",
            last_verified_on: "",
          }, intricacy: IntricacyHigh, volume: VolumeMedium, repo: "gunbc"),
        ],
        edges: [
          RoadmapEdge { child: nid(s: "2-stateless-frontend"), parent: nid(s: "2-cd-transport") },
        ],
      ),
      section_group(
        label: "SCM — source-intent integration, visibility-first (operator-directed 2026-07-25; premise revised 2026-07-27)",
        nodes: [
          with_plan(rn: ticket_row(id: "2-scm-product-strategy", done: false, t: TicketFields {
            headline: "product/adoption gate — prove agent landing before hosted SCM",
            brief: "Preserve the hosted Daglang developer-substrate destination while shrinking the first adoption ask: Git-compatible agent landing first, existing forge/Git target/editor/CI retained. Git is the first product realization, not the technical interface: independent Git, Mercurial, and Pijul models precede shared compatibility extraction. Promotion is evidence-ordered: A0 gunbc benchmark → A1 read-only shadow → A2 managed Git → A3 native proposals → A4 explicit hosted-native authority → A5 separate adjacent products.",
            displaced_cost: "Asking an unproven ecosystem to replace language, SCM, forge, editor, CI, and hosting at once; and spending reliability/privacy/compliance work on hosted authority before the integration advantage or customer pull is proven.",
            current_state: "Strategy PR #7336 and the C0 interaction carrier PR #7334 are merged. Commercial decisions remain open; no hosted source authority or forge replacement is authorized. Next technical work is the three upstream-model slices plus shared evidence, followed by compatibility-shape extraction and the bounded kernel.",
            first_slice: "A0 preparation: independently model bounded Git, Mercurial, and Pijul fixtures, extract only their proven common realization roles, then replay one real gunbc high-concurrency workload through the Git baseline and bounded Daglang path under the same safety contract. Measure choices, wrong results, invalidated validation, wall time, human minutes, and agent tokens; recruit one receipt-literate read-only design partner in parallel.",
            red_control: "The benchmark must include nearby always-apply and always-refuse falsifiers, attribute ordinary queue/cache/refresh wins separately from semantic wins, and publish refusals as well as successes. A1 writes no source ref; raw customer source cannot leave its declared placement or become training/secondary-use data by default.",
            out_of_scope: "No native object store, forge UI, hosted-authority flip, general-language semantic merge, customer-code data moat, product-grade Mercurial/Pijul promise without partner evidence, or assumption that word of mouth removes design-partner/sales work. Calendar, symmetry, sunk code, cheap serving, and conflict-count reduction cannot promote a stage.",
            handback: "Operator rules the eight decisions in the strategy plan: first forge surface, open/checkable boundary, partner count, pricing unit, language scope, authority-flip gate, customer-data rule, and review surface.",
            updates: [
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-27", note: "operator preserved hosted SCM as the long-term vertical-integration route, accepted a design-partner wedge, and requested an explicit strategy/milestone document before deciding what happens when" },
              TicketUpdate { on: "2026-07-27", note: "operator merged the strategy and C0 carrier, then fixed the next architecture constraint: Git may lead adoption but no Git-derived interface may become generic; Git, Mercurial, and Pijul stress the boundary before extraction" },
            ],
            authored_on: "2026-07-27",
            last_verified_on: "",
          }), path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-product-strategy.md"),
          with_plan(rn: ticket_wi(id: "2-scm-git-upstream-model", done: false, t: TicketFields {
            headline: "Git upstream fidelity model — complete its boundary before adapter extraction",
            brief: "Model Git as Git in `extdeps/`: blob/tree/commit/tag objects, tree-entry modes, raw paths, worktree/index, refs and symbolic refs, object/hash format, unavailable objects, and the exact-old-object `update-ref` guarantee. The bounded fixture captures, projects, and independently reads back its declared fragment without defining a common SCM interface.",
            displaced_cost: "An incomplete Git model silently loses executable bits, symlinks, gitlinks, tags, paths, or object-format facts; deriving the shared interface from that incomplete first realization would then cement both the omissions and Git's topology into every adapter.",
            current_state: "`extdeps.git`, `object_store`, and `versioning` exist, but `TreeEntry` lacks modes and `StoreObject` lacks tag objects; the full fidelity and conditional-advance boundary is not yet one witnessed upstream model.",
            first_slice: "DFS the existing Git extdeps, then model the missing object/tree/ref/fidelity roles from primary Git authorities. Add one bounded repository fixture that decodes, projects, independently reads back, and exercises exact-old-object advance as a Git capability receipt.",
            red_control: "Ordinary versus executable blob, symlink, gitlink, and annotated tag cannot collapse; a stale expected OID refuses before mutation; unavailable objects never decode as empty; the slice adds no `ScmKind`, generic adapter, proof-kernel branch, or Mercurial/Pijul alias.",
            out_of_scope: "No semantic integration kernel, forge/API transport, product hosting, inferred rename identity, universal branch/ref abstraction, or common compatibility carrier.",
